  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nHAZARDOUS WEATHER POSSIBLE THROUGH FRIDAY: \n\nRemnant moisture from a tropical cyclone formerly known as \nTrudy will move into the Four Corners tomorrow before tracking \nnortheastward across eastern Utah and western Colorado through \nFriday morning. A plethora of weather hazards are possible with \nthis system including flooding, flash flooding, strong to severe\nthunderstorms, and snow above 12,000-13,000 feet. As a result, \na Flood watch has been issued for parts of southeast Utah and \nsouthwest Colorado from 12 PM Wednesday through 12 PM Friday. \n\nWednesday:\n\nShowers and storms develop along the moisture transport \ngradient as early as tomorrow morning, with storms strengthening\nthroughout the afternoon. This initial push of convection \ntomorrow will pose a localized threat for heavy rainfall and \nflash flooding if stronger storms develop, especially if storms \nbegin to train over the same areas. Isolated severe storms are \npossible in the Four Corners region capable of producing large \nhail, damaging wind, and brief tornadoes as well due to the \npresence of modest instability and strong shear. However, the \nhighest potential for severe weather will remain off to our \nsouthwest in northwest into central Arizona. Stay weather aware \nif you have outdoor activities planned! If you experience any of\nthe hazards noted above, please report them to NWS Grand \nJunction.\n\nWednesday night - Thursday night: \n\nWith an anomalously moist tropical airmass overhead, widespread\nrainfall takes hold of the forecast area Wednesday night. \nPlease see the Hydrology Section below for more information \nconcerning the threat for flooding from this period of moderate \nto widespread rainfall. \n\nAnother round of strong to severe storms cannot be ruled out on\nThursday afternoon/evening with marginal instability and strong\nwind shear present. However, widespread cloud cover adds a \nlayer of uncertainty to the forecast and may inhibit surface \nheating. If stronger storms develop, isolated damaging wind, \nlarge hail, brief tornadoes are possible. We have low confidence\nin this aspect of the forecast. \n\nFriday: \n\nModels have continued to key in on a dry slot quickly \nevacuating the tropical moisture from the region on Friday \nmorning. Even after it stops raining, the risk for flooding and \nflash flooding will remain elevated on Friday morning as water \ncontinues to work through the system. Do not let your guard down\nright away! We'll have a better idea of how long flooding may \ncontinue to cause issues after we get a better sense of how much\nrainfall is received on Thursday. \n\n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nChances for light precipitation (valley rain, mountain snow \nabove 10,000 feet) continue through the weekend as a low \npressure system, currently spinning off the California coast, \ntraverses the Intermountain West. Plenty of uncertainty remains \nin how this low will evolve as it moves inland, but ensemble \nguidance tends to show a weakening system by the time it reaches\nour forecast area. Regardless, this system will provide enough \nforcing for scattered to widespread precipitation as it moves \nthrough, alongside a brief reduction in temperatures.",
